WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.540
- [Narrator] Shrinking
reservoirs, depleted aquifers,

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.830
drying rivers.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.190
These images reveal the
scale of the mega drought

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.020
sweeping the American West.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.090
It's the worst drought the region has seen

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.190
in at least 1,200 years.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.170
With more pressure on water supplies,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.520
one desert city offers a
promising conservation model.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.510
- You fly into Vegas,

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.580
you see the Bellagio Fountains,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.680
and you see the golf courses,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.770
and it looks like a
land of absolute waste.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.470
I've discovered that,
actually, Southern Nevada

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.830
is one of the most
water-minded places on Earth.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.390
- [Narrator] Since 2002,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.920
Southern Nevada has cut its
Colorado River water use by 26%

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.420
while also adding 750,000
people to its population.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.020
Still, severe drought conditions
are forcing the region

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.080
to find even more ways to
reduce its consumption.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.560
With the Colorado River Basin
at a dangerous tipping point,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.110
here's how Las Vegas offers a roadmap

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.573
to making the most out
of every drop of water.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.220
In June, the federal government

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.940
ordered unprecedented water
cutbacks asking Western states

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.910
to reduce their water
use by as much as 30%

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.220
to save the Colorado River system.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.620
- The Colorado River
really is the lifeblood

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.090
of the American Southwest.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.340
It runs for over 1,400 miles.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.240
And along the way, it provides
water for 40 million people.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.920
- [Narrator] As one of
the thirstiest cities

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.540
in the nation's driest state,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.060
Las Vegas leaders realized
they couldn't afford

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.800
to gamble on water.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.590
On average, Nevada receives
less than 10 inches

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.120
of rainfall a year.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.250
It also gets the smallest amount of water

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.020
from the Colorado River.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.940
- The Colorado River was divided up

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.380
about a hundred years ago

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.560
under the 1922 Colorado River Compact

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.310
where you had seven states
that were all negotiating

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.310
for how much water each
state would be allocated

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.660
from the river system.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.200
At that time, with the
population that we had,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.540
with the lack of potential for agriculture

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.820
here in our community sort of made sense

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.180
that perhaps Nevada did
only get a small amount.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.490
- [Narrator] Decades later,
scientists discovered

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.010
the compact severely overestimated

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.370
how much water flows down
the Colorado River each year.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.290
This over-allocation of water

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.030
combined with warming temperatures

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.380
and record-breaking drought

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.020
has the river system on
the verge of collapse.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.210
At the same time, the constant pressure

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.900
on Las Vegas' water supply has
helped transform the region

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.094
into a leader in conservation.

00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:32.430
(upbeat music)

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.340
The Bellagio Water Fountain
on the Las Vegas Strip

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.150
is one of the city's most
iconic and noticeable symbols

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.950
of excess water use.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.380
The 8.5 acre man-made lake

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.260
holds more than 22
million gallons of water,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.660
but nearly all of it is recycled.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.980
- The thing that most people don't know

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.020
is that the Bellagio Fountain doesn't use

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.850
any of our community's water supply.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.550
There is no Colorado
River water that is used

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.600
for that fountain.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.590
It is fed by a private
well that pulls groundwater

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.780
and is owned by the hotel.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.780
- [Narrator] Still, the
recycling system isn't perfect.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.450
The fountain could lose
millions of gallons annually

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.860
to evaporation.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.680
An MGM resort spokesperson
said they were committed

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.650
to using water responsibly
across the company

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:17.703
and supply chain.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.090
- The Las Vegas Strip
obviously is the backbone

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.250
of our community, but
what's really interesting

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.860
is that it only uses about 5%

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.230
of our community's total water supply.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.380
Residents who live here use 60%

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.450
of our community's total water supply.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.490
So I don't know any other community

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.060
that makes a very small
investment of its water supply

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.580
to get such great economic returns.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.720
- [Narrator] The Bellagio Fountain

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.340
represents just a fraction

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.800
of the region's water recycling efforts.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.080
Nearly every drop of water used indoors

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.840
throughout Southern Nevada
is treated, recycled,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.530
and returned in near-drinkable
quality to Lake Mead

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.040
according to the Clark County
Water Reclamation District.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.220
In 2021, this accounted for about 50%

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.470
of the region's total water consumption.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.620
- So the amount of water that
the Las Vegas Valley recycles

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.250
is absolutely unprecedented
in the Western United States.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.290
- [Narrator] Dan Fischer
works at the largest

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.710
water recycling plant on
the Colorado River Basin.

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.540
- For every gallon of
water that we return,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.430
we can take another gallon of water out.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.580
Without our ability to recycle our water

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.010
and safely return it to
the lake and reuse it

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.370
because we treat it so
effectively, without those things,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.710
Las Vegas wouldn't be what it is.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.050
- [Narrator] To keep up with
rapid population growth,

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.390
the region's water recycling facilities

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.460
are nearly always under construction.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.700
- The wastewater treatment
plants cannot get behind.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.350
That is just not an option.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.640
We have to stay ahead of it.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.580
We have to keep building
more and more facilities

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.560
in order to be able to take that water

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.040
and effectively treat it

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.400
- [Narrator] Another way Las Vegas

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.350
is able to cut back on its water

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:59.430
is by removing one of its
biggest water guzzlers, grass.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.630
- When you consider that
grass consumes 73 gallons

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.900
per square foot to survive
in the Mojave Desert,

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.960
you know, that's a column of
water that is 10 feet tall

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.570
for every square foot of grass.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.540
- [Narrator] In 1999, the
Southern Nevada Water Authority

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.120
started its cash for grass program,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.390
which currently pays homeowners
$3 for each square foot

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.980
of grass that's replaced

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.530
with water-efficient desert landscaping.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:26.760
The region has removed

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.790
about 207 million square feet of grass

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.770
as a result of this policy.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.810
- No place, really, in the American West

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.870
has been better than Las
Vegas at getting rid of grass.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.910
It's spread to the rest of the West.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.080
Phoenix is doing this.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.450
Los Angeles now has
kind of a war on grass,

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.250
but Las Vegas really is the pioneer.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.740
- [Narrator] More recently,
a new law in Southern Nevada,

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.600
the first of its kind in the nation,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.640
bans non-functional grass.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.740
That's the grass in medians and roadways

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.590
that is only planted
for decorative purposes.

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.110
Ecologists say grass lawns can also be bad

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.870
for desert ecosystems not meant to sustain

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.330
water-intensive plants.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.660
- As of today, there are
approximately 3,000 acres

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.520
of non-functional grass.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.370
Replacing that grass will save about 10%

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.473
of our community's overall water supply.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.560
- [Narrator] Rigorous conservation efforts

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.160
don't just outlaw grass, but
also dictate when residents

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.400
can water their yards or wash their cars.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.730
Water investigators like
this one patrol neighborhoods

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.220
to cite people who violate rules.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.180
- We leave a courtesy door
hanger with a watering guide

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.480
letting them know, "Hey you're
not allowed to water today."

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.800
- I think it's important to note

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.530
that Southern Nevada did
not place conservation

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:41.730
on one sector.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.130
We did not just require it of the resorts

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.420
down on the Las Vegas Strip.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:47.790
We did not just require it of residents.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:51.660
We placed conservation on the
backs of every single sector

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.400
in our community.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.140
- [Narrator] As the drought
across the American West

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.870
worsens, reservoirs across the region

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.760
are reaching critical levels.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.880
This summer, the country's
largest reservoir, Lake Mead,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.410
fell to a record low.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:05.970
- You see the pictures of Lake Mead,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:09.780
you see the 200 feet of air
where there used to be water

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:10.920
for over the entire lake.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.170
Unless the drought subsides,

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.930
we could get to a point where
Lake Mead cannot deliver

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.730
any water out of Hoover Dam.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:22.470
That's a real possibility and
that's a terrifying thought.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:23.550
- [Narrator] The federal government

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.670
says the seven Colorado River Basin states

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.310
still need to make more water cutbacks.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.810
- And the Feds basically said,

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.020
if you don't do it voluntarily,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.162
we reserve the right to make you,

00:07:36.162 --> 00:07:37.710
we'll decide for you.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:38.910
- [Narrator] All the basin states

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.920
said they recognized their shared role

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.260
in bringing the river system into balance.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.980
More recently, California, the
region's largest water user

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.670
promised to reduce the state's
use of Colorado River water

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:55.320
by 400,000 acre feet every
year for the next three years.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.660
Even as a leader in water conservation,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.640
the Southern Nevada Water Authority

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.180
says Las Vegas is finding
more ways to cut back.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.210
- It takes all sectors of your community

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.520
in order to contribute
to water conservation

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.810
and be successful.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.540
And it's going to be the same
thing on the Colorado River.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.440
It is going to take every
water user across every sector

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.420
to be contributing to the efforts

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.100
to reduce those water demands,

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.071
so that we can bring this
system back into balance.

00:08:23.071 --> 00:08:25.654
(gentle music)

